Mark XIX: profiles + export/import

- Profiles are fully isolated per identity: brain config, memories,
  conversations, health data, reminders, board, watch rules, license and
  logs. The pre-existing single-profile directory IS the "default" profile
  - nothing moves, migration is a no-op, no data loss. Other profiles live
  at <base>/profiles/<name>/; every module already resolves
  config.config_dir() dynamically, so isolation is automatic. The active
  profile is recorded in <base>/profile; config.using_profile() provides a
  scoped override for export/import and tests.
- jarvis profile list | create <name> | use <name> | delete <name> [--yes]:
  name validation (1-32 chars, letters/digits/-/_), the default profile can
  never be deleted, the active profile cannot be deleted under itself,
  deletion walks the tree explicitly (no shutil.rmtree).
- jarvis export [--profile name] [--out file] [--include-secrets] and
  jarvis import <file.json> [--yes] with EXPORT_SCHEMA = 1: the importer
  refuses unknown schema versions. Secrets are excluded by default;
  --include-secrets requires an explicit interactive confirmation and the
  file is chmod 600 on POSIX. License keys are never exported. Import
  replaces the active profile's config and MERGES memories + conversations
  (never destroys existing data).
- config.py: _base_dir()/active_profile()/set_active_profile()/using_profile();
  existing config_dir() semantics preserved for the default profile.
- Tests: profile isolation (default == base dir, memory separation,
  switch round-trip, deletion guards, name validation) and export/import
  (schema, no secrets by default, file round-trip into a fresh profile,
  include_secrets carries keys, unknown schema refused).
- Version 3.6.0.
